UPDATE posts SET content = $tag8$<p>Today I would like to talk to you about Camille Claudel, who is known as a sculptor beyond her time.</p>



<p> Camille's father was a banker and her mother was the daughter of a wealthy Catholic family. His brother and future supporter, Paul Claudel, was a playwright, poet and diplomat. Born in France in 1864 and unknown in the golden age of her art, Camille became truly known in the 20th century. It was impossible to evaluate his works objectively in the male-dominated society he lived in, because at that time even women were forbidden to take classes in art academies. Women artists had to take classes in private workshops, as they were forbidden to take formal classes. This is how Camille met the famous sculptor Auguste Rodin (1883). Camille became a student of Rodin. Rodin was 43 and Camille just 19 when they met. With an imagination and talent that quickly stood out from Rodin's other students, Camille became Rodin's favorite student.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Camille_Claudel.jpg" alt="Camille Claudel" class="wp-image-6072" width="616" height="763"/><figcaption><em>Camille Claudel</em></figcaption></figure>



<h2>Rodin and Camille Claudel</h2>



<p>Together they laid the foundation for many of Rodin's works, and Claudel became indispensable to Rodin and his art. Even now, the works of Auguste Rodin are divided into 'Before Camille &amp; After Camille'. Challenging years began for Camille with the start of a romantic relationship between them. When he met Rodin and Camille, Rodin had a 20-year relationship and a son from that relationship. According to some sources, Rodin was married.</p>



<p>The relationship between them started to turn into a problem as the years passed. In the art community, the Camille breeze in Rodin's works stood out. Some critics even took it a step further, pointing out that Rodin was "a little too much" inspired by Camille. Indeed, there were traces of Camille in Rodin's most important works. He made Rodin's famous "Gates of Hell" together with Rodin and Camille, but Camille's name was never fully mentioned with this work. An unnamed rivalry began between Rodin and Camille. This secret rivalry turned into an ambition for Rodin, who was not mentioned well in relationship matters anyway.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/the-gates-of-hell-654x1024.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-6073" width="327" height="512"/><figcaption><em>Aguste Rodin, The Gates of Hell</em></figcaption></figure>



<p>Camille left home because her mother did not approve of this relationship. While trying to make his name known, he was overshadowed by Rodin and was going through a difficult process after losing his baby. Camille left Rodin many times, but they made up again. This process continued like this. Until 1889, when Camille left Rodin once and for all.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/the-walts-asil-2.jpg" alt="Camille Claudel , The Waltzs" class="wp-image-6078" width="207" height="255"/><figcaption>         <em>Camille Claudel, The Waltzs  </em>                                                                     </figcaption></figure>



<p>Camille struggled to survive as a solo artist after this breakup. He produced the best works of his artistic life after he broke up with Rodin, but his works apart from Rodin were rejected and condemned by the society. While the works of Auguste Rodin continue to be praised, his private life has never hindered his career. However, art circles turned their backs on Camille and did not accept her works that she made alone. His brother, who was his only supporter, was in China. Camille, who was isolated in every aspect of society, destroyed most of her works in one night.</p>



<p>After this incident, he was closed to a psychiatric hospital in 1913. According to some sources, Camille's closure to the hospital was the decision taken by her mother and Rodin together. In this hospital, he only corresponded with his brother Paul. In his letters, he stated more than once that he thought Rodin was responsible for his confinement to a mental institution, as well as the pain of not being able to practice his art. During the years he was in the hospital, he was not given the opportunity to make a sculpture. It is said that in the letter her doctor wrote to her family, Camille emphasized the need to return home and sculpt, but her mother did not respond to this letter. Camille Claudel stayed in the hospital for 33 years and died there in 1943.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/the-mature-age-1024x722.jpg" alt="Camille Claudel Olgunluk Çağı (The Mature Age)" class="wp-image-6074" width="577" height="403"/><figcaption><em>Camille Claudel, The Mature Age</em></figcaption></figure>



<p>I think it must have been easy for everyone to see the reactions he gave after all the pain, loneliness and exploitation of his labor as 'a woman's hysteria, insanity'. Was it not crazy that Camille did not endure the oppression of women throughout her life and wanted to make her talent and name known, sculpturing as a 'male profession'?</p>



<p>While talking about Camille's life and works, I would not want to talk about Auguste Rodin, who is rumored to still portray some of his works as if he had done them, and who is certain to exploit his work. However, Camille Claudel is one of the most prominent examples in the art world of benefiting from women's labor, ignoring their success, and gaining benefits by using social norms on women.</p>



<p>The movie "Camille Claudel", which was released in 1988 and nominated for 2 Oscars, made it known outside the art circle.</p>



<p><em>Source:</em></p>



<p><em>Camille Claudel &amp; Rodin: Fateful Encounter, Antoinette le Normand-Romain</em></p>



<p><em>Camille Claudel/Bir Kadın, Anne Delbee</em></p>



<p>Diğer yazılarımızı okumak için <a href="https://www.loveinartsz.com">anasayfayı</a> ziyaret etmeyi unutmayın.</p>$tag8$ WHERE slug = 'a-sculptor-beyond-the-age-camille-claudel';
UPDATE posts SET content = $tag8$<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Y%C4%B1ld%C4%B1zl%C4%B1-Gece.jpg" alt=""/></figure>



<p>Vincent van Gogh was a Dutch post-impressionist painter who is among the most famous and influential figures in the history of Western art. His paintings, including his renowned work The Starry Night, have become icons of modern art.</p>



<p>Van Gogh was born in 1853 in the Netherlands and showed an early interest in art. However, his family discouraged his artistic pursuits and he did not begin seriously studying art until he was in his late twenties. Despite his late start, he quickly developed his unique style and became one of the leading figures of the post-impressionist movement.</p>



<p>One of van Gogh's most famous works is The Starry Night, which he painted in 1889 while living in an asylum in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, France. The painting depicts a moonlit night with swirling clouds, a glowing crescent moon, and a small town nestled below. The sky is filled with stars, including a large, bright star that dominates the composition.</p>



<p>The Starry Night is a prime example of van Gogh's distinctive style, which is characterized by bold, thick brushstrokes and vibrant colors. His use of swirling lines and radiant hues imbues the painting with a sense of movement and energy. The painting's subject matter is also typical of van Gogh's work, as he often depicted the beauty of the natural world and the humbleness of rural life.</p>



<p>Despite its beauty and iconic status, The Starry Night was not well-received when it was first exhibited. Critics and audiences alike were unimpressed by van Gogh's bold use of color and his unconventional techniques. As a result, the painting did not sell and van Gogh continued to struggle with poverty and mental illness throughout his life.</p>



<p>It was only after van Gogh's death in 1890 that his work began to gain recognition and appreciation. In the decades following his death, van Gogh's paintings, including The Starry Night, were exhibited in major museums and galleries around the world. Today, van Gogh is considered one of the greatest painters in history and The Starry Night is one of the most famous paintings in the world.</p>



<p>The Starry Night is currently on display at the Museum of Modern Art in New York City. The painting has been the subject of numerous reproductions and parodies, and its distinctive style and subject matter have made it an enduring symbol of van Gogh's legacy.</p>



<p>Despite the hardships he faced during his lifetime, van Gogh's talent and vision have had a lasting impact on the world of art. His paintings, including The Starry Night, continue to inspire and captivate audiences today, making him one of the most beloved and revered artists in history.</p>$tag8$ WHERE slug = 'van-goghs-starry-night';
UPDATE posts SET content = $tag8$<p>Art and soul are two concepts that are often intertwined and are essential aspects of the human experience. Art is a form of expression that allows individuals to creatively convey their thoughts, feelings, and ideas through various mediums, such as painting, music, dance, and literature. The soul, on the other hand, is often seen as the core of an individual's being, their innermost essence, and the source of their emotions and consciousness.</p>



<p>The connection between art and soul is deep and complex, with each influencing and enriching the other. For many, creating art is a way to connect with their soul and tap into their innermost thoughts and feelings. Through art, individuals can explore their emotions, express their creativity, and connect with their deepest selves.</p>



<p>For some, the act of creating art is a spiritual experience, as it allows them to connect with a higher power or a universal energy. This connection can be deeply profound and can provide a sense of peace and fulfillment. For others, art is a way to explore the mysteries of the soul and to better understand the human experience.</p>



<p>The relationship between art and soul is also evident in the way that art can affect the viewer. When an individual views a piece of art, they may be struck by its beauty, its emotional power, or its ability to speak to their soul. Art has the ability to move people and to inspire them, to lift their spirits and to provide comfort in times of need.</p>



<p>In many ways, the creation of art and the exploration of the soul are intertwined. Both require a deep level of self-reflection and introspection, as well as the willingness to be vulnerable and open to new ideas and experiences. Through art, individuals can learn more about themselves and the world around them, and they can discover new depths of meaning and purpose in their lives.</p>



<p>One of the most beautiful aspects of art is its ability to bring people together and to foster a sense of community and connection. When individuals create art together, they can share in the joy and fulfillment of the creative process, and they can support and inspire each other. This sense of connection and community can be incredibly nourishing for the soul, providing individuals with a sense of belonging and purpose.</p>



<p>In conclusion, art and soul are deeply connected, with each influencing and enriching the other. Art provides a way for individuals to connect with their innermost selves and to explore their emotions and creativity. The soul, in turn, can be nourished and enriched by the act of creating art and by the experience of viewing art. Whether through the creation of art or through its appreciation, the relationship between art and soul is a powerful and enduring one.</p>$tag8$ WHERE slug = 'art-and-soul';
UPDATE posts SET content = $tag8$<p>Rococo art is a style of art that emerged in the 18th century and is characterized by its ornate and elaborate designs, as well as its focus on intricate details, pastel colors, and themes of love, romance, and the leisurely pursuits of the aristocracy.</p>



<p>The word "rococo" is derived from the French words "rocaille" and "coquille," which refer to the shells and pebbles that were often used to decorate the interiors of Rococo-style buildings. This decorative style is often associated with the period of European history known as the Enlightenment, which was a time of great cultural, scientific, and artistic achievement.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/RococoArt.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-9954"/></figure>



<p>Rococo art is often seen as a reaction against the grandeur and formalism of the preceding Baroque style. Instead of the grand, dramatic themes and bold colors of Baroque art, Rococo artists favored a more delicate and lighthearted approach, with a focus on the beauty of nature, the pleasures of love and romance, and the leisurely pursuits of the aristocracy.</p>



<p>One of the most famous Rococo artists was Jean-Antoine Watteau, who is known for his elegant and graceful paintings of outdoor gatherings, musical performances, and other scenes of everyday life. Watteau's work was a major influence on other Rococo artists, and his style is often seen as the epitome of the Rococo aesthetic.</p>



<p>Another notable Rococo artist was François Boucher, who was known for his colorful and highly detailed paintings of mythological and allegorical subjects. Boucher's work was highly sought after by the aristocracy, and he was a favorite of King Louis XV of France. His paintings are often seen as a celebration of the pleasures and leisure of the French court.</p>



<p>Rococo art was not just limited to painting, however. The style also influenced other art forms, such as sculpture, architecture, and decorative arts. For example, the renowned French sculptor Jean-Baptiste Pigalle was a leading figure in the Rococo movement, and his work is known for its delicate and intricate details, as well as its playful and lighthearted spirit.</p>



<p>In architecture, the Rococo style is best exemplified by the Palace of Versailles, which was built by King Louis XIV in the 17th century but was later renovated and redecorated in the Rococo style. The palace is famous for its ornate and elaborate decorations, as well as its extensive use of mirrors and other reflective surfaces, which were intended to create the illusion of an even larger and more luxurious space.</p>



<p>Despite its popularity during the 18th century, Rococo art fell out of favor in the 19th century, as many people came to see it as overly frivolous and ornate. However, in recent years, there has been a resurgence of interest in Rococo art, and many people are once again appreciating its beauty and elegance.</p>



<p>In conclusion, Rococo art is a style of art that emerged in the 18th century and is characterized by its ornate and elaborate designs, pastel colors, and focus on love, romance, and the leisurely pursuits of the aristocracy. This style had a major influence on many different art forms, and its legacy can still be seen in the works of many modern artists.</p>$tag8$ WHERE slug = 'what-is-rococo-art';
UPDATE posts SET content = $tag8$<p>Mitolojik bir karakter olmasa da Pygmalion, hikayesiyle oldukça ilgi çeken bir kahramandır. Pygmalion kendi yonttuğu heykele aşık olmasıyla tanınır. Bir yontu sanatçısı olan Pygmalion'un hikayesi <a href="https://www.loveinartsz.com/mitoloji-tarih/" title="">mitolojide</a> önemli bir yere sahiptir. Aynı zamanda sanat dünyasında da bu hikayeden ilham alınarak oluşturulan bir çok eser bulunur.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Chateau_de_Versailles_salon_des_nobles_Pygmalion_priant_Venus_danimer_sa_statue_Jean-Baptiste_Regnault-1-1024x770.jpg" alt="Pygmalion priant Vénus d'animer sa statue, Jean-Baptiste Regnault" class="wp-image-9966" width="600"/><figcaption>Pygmalion priant Vénus d'animer sa statue, Jean-Baptiste Regnault</figcaption></figure>



<h2>Pygmalion Kimdir?</h2>



<p>Kıbrıs'ta yaşayan bir mermer ustası ve heykeltraştır. Aynı adı taşıyan Fenike Kralı Pygmalion'un da etkisiyle yontucu kral olarak anılır. Mütevaziliği ve işinde ustalığı ile öne çıkan Pygmalion'un yaşadığı yerdeki kadınlar Venüs tarafından cezalandırılmıştı. Bu cezanın nedeni kadınları tanrıça Venüs'e gereken ilgi ve saygıyı göstermemeleriydi. Tanrıça kadınların bu tavrı nedeniyle onları cezalandırmış ve ahlak yoksunu kadınlar galine getirmişti.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full is-resized"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Pygmalion__Galatea_Louis_Gauffier-1.jpg" alt="Pygmalion ve Galatea, Louis Gauffier, 1797" class="wp-image-9965" width="600"/><figcaption><em>Pygmalion ve Galatea</em>, <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Louis_Gauffier&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">Louis Gauffier</a>, 1797</figcaption></figure>



<h2>Kendi Yaptığı Heykele Aşık Olan Heykeltıraş</h2>



<p>Pygmalion, yaşadığı yerdeki kadınların cezalandırılması nedeniyle kendisine uygun eş bulamıyordu. Bu durum canını sıkıyor ve işine dört kolla sarılmasını sağlıyordu.</p>



<p>Günlerden bir gün Pygmalion, kendisi için bir heykel yapmaya karar verdi. Kendisi için ideal kadını yontmak için mermeri yontmaya başladı. İşini öyle tutkuyla yapıyordu ki mermer elinin altında can bulacak gibiydi.</p>



<p>Nihayet heykel tamamlandığında o kadar güzel bir kadın ile karşılaştı ki hemen bu eşsiz mermer kadına güzel elbiseler giydirerek süsledi.</p>



<p>Pygmalion, yonttuğu heykele aşık olmuştu. Tanrılara dua etmeye başladı: "Siz tanrılar madem ki her şeyi yapacak güçtesiniz, bana da bir eş verin, fakat ne olur heykelime benzesin." Venüs bu duayı çok içten buldu ve duası kabul oldu. Mermer hayat bularak Pygmalion'a eş oldu.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized"><img src="https://www.loveinartsz.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Etienne-Maurice_Falconet_-_Pygmalion_and_Galatea_-_Walters_27387_-_View_A-1-857x1024.jpg" alt="Pygmalion canlanan heykelinin ayakları dibinde, Étienne Maurice Falconet" class="wp-image-9964" width="600"/><figcaption><em>Pygmalion canlanan heykelinin ayakları dibinde</em>, Étienne Maurice Falconet</figcaption></figure>



<h2>Pygmalion Hikayesi Hangi Eserde Yer Alıyor?</h2>



<p>Mitolojik bir karakter olan Pygmalion'un hikayesi "Romalı ozan <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ovidius">Ovidius</a>'un <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%B6n%C3%BC%C5%9F%C3%BCmler">Metamorphoses (Dönüşümler)</a> adlı yapıtının 10. kitabında anlatılmaktadır (243-298 dizeler). Kadının ismi bu eserde anılmamaktadır. Ancak klasik dönem sanat ve yazınında ona deniz <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nymphaea">nympha’ları</a> <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nereid">Nereid</a>'lerden biri olan <em>Galatea</em>’nın ismi verilmiştir. <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goethe">Goethe</a> ise, ona mitolojik <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kartaca">Kartaca</a> şehrini kuran kraliçesi <a href="https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dido_(Kartaca_Krali%C3%A7esi)">Dido</a>’nun diğer ismi olan <em>Elise</em> ismini vermiştir."</p>



<p></p>



